The `poll` function lacks error handling. If the API call fails, it will throw an unhandled error which could crash the polling mechanism. Similar patterns in the codebase (e.g., `context/kv.tsx`, `context/theme.tsx`) use `.catch(() => {})` to handle errors gracefully. Consider adding error handling to prevent the timer from stopping on transient network failures.
```suggestion
      try {
        const sessionID = route.data.type === "session" ? route.data.sessionID : undefined
        const result = await sdk.client.tui.statusline({ sessionID })
        if (!result.data) return
        setTemplates(result.data.templates)
        setFrequency(result.data.interval)
      } catch {
        // Ignore errors to keep polling running on transient failures
      }
```

`clipboardy` is always added as a copy method, even when a native method (wl-copy/xclip/xsel/powershell/etc.) is enabled. Since clipboardy typically shells out to the same underlying tools, this can cause redundant attempts (and duplicated errors) before failing, and can add extra latency. Consider only adding the clipboardy fallback when no OS-specific methods were added, or otherwise de-duplicating so each backend is tried at most once.
```suggestion
    if (list.length === 0) {
      list.push({
        name: "clipboardy",
        copy: async (text: string) => {
          await clipboardy.write(text)
        },
      })
    }
```

When the JWT exchange request fails (line 48), the function silently falls back to returning the raw OAuth token without logging the failure. This makes debugging authentication issues difficult, especially for GitHub Enterprise deployments where the JWT exchange is required.

Consider logging the error response (status code and message) before falling back, so administrators can diagnose configuration or connectivity issues with their Enterprise instance. This would have helped debug the issue described in #3936 more quickly.
```suggestion
  if (!response.ok) {
    let errorText = ""
    try {
      errorText = await response.text()
    } catch {
      errorText = "<failed to read error response body>"
    }
    console.error(
      `[copilot] Failed to exchange OAuth token for Copilot JWT for domain "${domain}" (status ${response.status} ${response.statusText}): ${errorText}`,
    )
    return oauthToken
  }
```

If downloadResponse.body is null or undefined, the code silently skips writing the file but continues to attempt extraction, which will fail. Consider adding an explicit check and error logging when response.body is missing, as this indicates a failure to download the archive.
```suggestion
        if (!downloadResponse.body) {
          log.error("Failed to download terraform-ls: response body is empty")
          return
        }

        const tempPath = path.join(Global.Path.bin, "terraform-ls.zip")
        await Filesystem.writeStream(tempPath, downloadResponse.body)
```
